# CVE-2026-46600: golang.org/x/net/dns/dnsmessage (x/net before 0.56.0).
# Newly disclosed sibling of the -46595/-46597 batch above; surfaced by a
# Trivy DB update on 2026-08-12.
# phase-820 CORRECTION: the previous note claimed "the same four Go-based
# monitoring images" and named haproxy-exporter (now deleted — see the Phase
# 810/820 block below). A no-ignorefile scan of every pinned image on
# 2026-08-14 shows the real affected set is wider: cadvisor:v0.52.1,
# promtail:3.6.11, alertmanager:v0.33.1, grafana:13.0.6-ubuntu, loki:3.7.6,
# prometheus:v3.13.2, node-exporter:v1.12.1, redis_exporter:v1.87.0 — AND
# ja4proxy:2.0.0, our own proxy image.
# Why no fix (third-party): pinned tags that compile x/net transitively;
# fixing requires an upstream rebuild, and none has shipped.
# ⚠ NOT TRUE OF ja4proxy:2.0.0 — that is a Go stdlib finding
# (deploy/docker/Dockerfile.go-proxy pins golang:1.26.5-alpine) and Go 1.26.6
# fixes it. This waiver must NOT be read as covering our own image; the bump
# is tracked as its own phase, together with splitting .trivyignore into
# first-party and third-party files so a sidecar waiver can never again
# silently suppress a finding in the product.
# Why not exploitable (third-party only): dns/dnsmessage parses
# host-originated DNS records, which none of those components reach in our
# deployment. Re-review at expiry.
CVE-2026-46600 exp:2026-08-23

# CVE-2026-56852: golang.org/x/text norm.Iter can enter an infinite loop on
# certain malformed input during Unicode normalization.
# Why no fix: newly disclosed; found during the 2026-07-30 re-verification
# scan that also bumped loki 3.7.2 -> 3.7.4. Present in all four Go-based
# monitoring images (alertmanager:v0.33.1, grafana:13.0.4-ubuntu,
# loki:3.7.4, promtail:3.6.11) via a shared golang.org/x/text v0.38.0
# transitive dependency; fix ships in 0.39.0 but no image has picked it up
# yet (confirmed against the newest published tag of each).
# Why not exploitable: norm.Iter (Unicode normalization) is not invoked on
# any attacker-controlled input path in our deployment — none of these four
# services expose an endpoint that normalizes untrusted text; they consume
# metrics/logs (already-structured data) or serve their own admin UI/API to
# authenticated internal users only.
CVE-2026-56852 exp:2026-08-23



# GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf: gRPC-Go xDS RBAC and HTTP/2 Vulnerabilities (fixed in
# grpc-go v1.82.1). Re-verified 2026-08-03: prometheus was bumped to v3.13.2
# in this same review, which picks up the fix (scanned clean, 0 findings) —
# removed from the affected list below. Still no upstream release of
# alertmanager (v0.33.1, latest), grafana (13.0.4-ubuntu, latest — checked
# 13.1.1-ubuntu too, still vulnerable plus a larger CVE set, not worth
# switching), loki (3.7.4, latest), promtail (v3.6.11, latest — EOL project),
# or gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor (v0.52.1 — checked v0.55.1, carries far more
# HIGH/CRITICAL CVEs, not an improvement) has picked up the fix.
# Why not exploitable here: the vulnerable code paths are gRPC's xDS RBAC
# filter and an HTTP/2 handling flaw used by Envoy-style xDS control planes.
# None of these five images run as xDS servers or accept untrusted gRPC/xDS
# input in our deployment — they're monitoring/logging tools sitting behind
# the proxy, reachable only from trusted internal callers.
GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf exp:2026-08-23

# ── gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor — Phase 810, narrowed by Phase 820 ──
